 "body_old": "Research suggests deep tissue massage added to conventional rehabilitation may reduce pain intensity and improve some neck-movement and quality-of-life measures in people with neck pain linked to tender spots in the muscles.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36645811/\">[1]</a> A randomized trial in athletes linked repeated deep tissue massage with improvements in flexibility and recovery-related measures, though the results may not apply to every client.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40510040/\">[2]</a> Trials in specific musculoskeletal conditions suggest deep tissue massage may improve pain or movement-related outcomes for some people, but results vary by condition and outcome, and long-term pain benefits remain uncertain.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36645811/\">[1]</a><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31655314/\">[3]</a><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29861215/\">[4]</a>",
 "body_new": "Research on deep tissue massage is limited and mixed. In a trial of 80 adults with neck pain linked to tender spots in the muscles, adding deep tissue massage to conventional rehabilitation was followed by more improvement in pain, quality-of-life scores and most neck movements than rehabilitation alone.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36645811/\">[1]</a> A larger trial of 619 adults with ongoing neck pain found that deep tissue massage led to more improvement in pain than advice to stay active in the short term but not by 12 months, and a review of the research found that studies differ too much in what they call deep tissue massage and how they do it to draw clear conclusions.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31655314/\">[2]</a><a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29861215/\">[3]</a> People given massage, exercise or both in that larger trial were also more likely than those given advice to say they had recovered, at every follow-up through one year.<a href=\"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31655314/\">[2]</a>",
